    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5119e519-de01-52eb-9413-81125a312fe0",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=1-t^{6}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=1-t^{6}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(1-t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(1-t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}-\\frac{t^{6+1}}{(6+1)}+C \\\\ &= t-\\frac{t^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a34842ac-0fb3-54c4-96f5-331029c94a1e",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
